WEBVTT

00:00:00.658 --> 00:00:28.530
<v Riley>Hey there, good to have you. Today on the show we've got a dispute over a $12.4 million federal audit, with the Federation of Sovereign Indigenous Nations heading to court. We're also bringing home a tipi cover that spent a century at the Smithsonian, and an update on residential school dates that's worth checking yourself. Then there's the Quesnel festival that needs three people before its November meeting. Stick around for the deep dive on who holds the records.

00:00:42.005 --> 00:01:01.981
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Thursday, October 15th, 2026. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation. Here in the Cariboo, on the traditional and unceded territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:01:02.981 --> 00:01:28.509
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're spending most of our time on who holds the decision when a nation, a funder, and a museum disagree about what belongs to whom. Quick weather check: out at Horsefly Lake it's overcast and around 2 this morning, with a high of 18, a low of 1, and no precipitation expected. Around the region, 100 Mile House is about 4, Williams Lake and Quesnel are about 2, and Redstone is about 0, all under overcast skies.

00:01:28.709 --> 00:01:29.517
<v Riley>On to the news.

00:01:30.917 --> 00:02:05.515
<v Riley>Most of today's stories land in our own backyard, and the provincial ballot turns up more than once. Starting in 100 Mile House. The South Cariboo Recreation Centre has added $10,000 in accessibility upgrades, according to the 100 Mile Free Press. The paper says a local initiative under the district made the town more accessible this past summer. The article is cut off before it lists which upgrades the money paid for, and that detail matters. A recreation centre is where many people with mobility limits meet the rest of town.

00:02:05.965 --> 00:02:10.205
<v Casey>Ten thousand dollars is a small line in a budget. Ask which doors it opened.

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:28.179
<v Casey>The Williams Lake Tribune's candidate profiles, which we covered on Monday, continue ahead of Saturday's general election on October 17th. Sheila Boehm is the latest city council candidate profiled. The profile is a candidate's own account, so the record behind any promise is a separate check.

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:32.477
<v Riley>Same questions for everyone makes the answers easier to compare.

00:02:34.041 --> 00:02:55.697
<v Casey>The Quesnel Community Foundation's annual gala is set for Saturday, October 24th, at 5:30 p.m. at the seniors centre. That's the same day British Columbians vote in the provincial election, so anyone with a ballot and a ticket will have a full day. My Cariboo Now's notice doesn't say whether tickets are still available, so check with the foundation before you plan around it.

00:02:57.121 --> 00:03:14.105
<v Riley>My Cariboo Now reports that the Thanksgiving food drive in Quesnel was a success, and that the results are in. The item doesn't include the totals. So we know it went well, but we don't know by how much. A food drive that doesn't publish its numbers asks the next volunteer group to guess.

00:03:15.506 --> 00:03:39.050
<v Casey>The future of Quesnel's Billy Barker Days is in doubt. The Quesnel Cariboo Observer reports that the festival's future will be decided at the society's annual general meeting on November 19th. The paper says the society is searching for people to fill three key positions, and Secretary and director Lynne Wright is quoted in the piece. The festival draws a summer crowd, but the crowd doesn't run the society.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:46.340
<v Riley>A festival that needs three specific people and one meeting date has a governance problem before a money problem.

00:03:47.831 --> 00:04:15.407
<v Casey>The B.C. NDP released a fully costed platform hours before a leaders' debate scheduled for Wednesday. The Williams Lake Tribune reports that the platform adds spending for projects and priorities, and adds new taxes on the wealthiest 3.88 per cent of residents. The party says the deficit stays on a track similar to the last provincial budget. The story doesn't say how much of the costing depends on projected growth. Growth assumptions are where a costed platform gets tested.

00:04:16.967 --> 00:04:45.415
<v Riley>The vote is Saturday, October 24th. We covered the Cariboo-Chilcotin field on Friday: Kathryn Askew for the NDP, Lorne Doerkson for the Conservatives, Douglas Gook for the Greens. In Prince George-North Cariboo, it's Denice Bardua for the NDP, Sheldon Clare for the Conservatives, and Randy Thompson for the Greens. We have not yet established an earlier provincial run for Askew, Bardua, or Thompson. All three parties should answer to the same costing test.

00:04:46.934 --> 00:05:10.294
<v Casey>Now the stories with a wider reach. APTN reports that First Nations child advocate Cindy Blackstock says a 20-year-old case against the federal government may be almost over. She also says the file has regressed since Mandy Gull-Masty became minister. That comes from an interview with APTN's Face to Face. The article doesn't say which specific steps she sees as backward, so one interview is carrying that claim.

00:05:11.728 --> 00:05:36.352
<v Riley>Several First Nations assemblies across the country are rejecting the Building Canada Strong Act, which APTN describes as the "one-project, one-year" bill. The bill has had its first vote. The article doesn't count the assemblies involved, so the scale of the opposition can't be measured from this one story. The objections do come from the assemblies themselves, not from a single advocacy group.

00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:42.602
<v Casey>A one-project, one-year bill sounds like a speed rule. Speed rules are where consultation gets squeezed.

00:05:44.037 --> 00:06:13.685
<v Riley>Nautilus reports what it calls the oldest peace treaty ever discovered. It's an agreement from around 1269 BCE between Ramses II of Egypt and Hittite King Hattusili III, to stop hostilities over disputed Syrian territories. Archaeologists have been piecing it together from fragments found in the Hittite capital for about 120 years, and they have just turned up another piece. The article says the lesson it leans on most is that two sides wrote the terms down.

00:06:15.234 --> 00:06:35.754
<v Casey>APTN reports that Dene leaders have passed an education resolution. It calls for stable funding, language programming, and student supports. It also pushes for a Dene-led approach to child and family services. The article doesn't say which Dene nations passed it. Language programs and child services in one resolution is a governance ask stacked on a funding ask.

00:06:37.171 --> 00:07:00.899
<v Riley>Microsoft says it helped take down a cybercrime network linked to more than a billion scam messages. Working with Japanese authorities, it disabled more than 20,000 accounts connected to a marketplace for scammers. The Verge says the takedown should mean fewer parking-ticket and fake-delivery texts. The company has not said how many of the messages reached Canadian phones.

00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:06.285
<v Casey>Takedowns tend to push the business somewhere else, and scam texts reach a rural phone first.

00:07:07.782 --> 00:07:29.958
<v Riley>Dezeen covers a house and workshop designed by Omar Gandhi Architects with furniture maker Brodye Chappell. It has a corrugated metal facade and sits in the agricultural area of Marshfield, just outside Charlottetown on Prince Edward Island. The architects say the building touches its surroundings lightly. It's a rural build with a clear material language.

00:07:30.408 --> 00:07:38.200
<v Casey>The money and governance questions are where the deep dive picks up, starting with a dispute over a federal audit. First, a community spotlight.

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:59.024
<v Riley>For more than twenty years, Denisiqi Services Society has delivered child and family services grounded in the culture of the Tŝilhqot'in and Ulkatcho communities. The service is built to answer to those communities first, and that's a standard worth knowing about. If you want to learn more, visit denisiqi dot org.

00:07:59.372 --> 00:08:02.668
<v Casey>Twenty years of showing up for families is the endorsement I'd give.

00:08:04.068 --> 00:08:25.172
<v Casey>The Federation of Sovereign Indigenous Nations says it rejects Indigenous Services Canada's final forensic audit decision, and it has launched legal action over $12.4 million in spending. ISC says that money is ineligible under the funding agreements. Some regional outlets put the figure at more than $12.3 million, so the number depends on who's reporting it.

00:08:25.769 --> 00:08:51.513
<v Riley>The number matters less than the sequence. An audit produces a decision. The decision produces a repayment demand. The nation takes it to court. FSIN says it denies wrongdoing and that it took part fully in the review, and a court will have to weigh that. The question under all of it is who decides what a First Nation's own books say, when the federal government holds the money, the audit, and the power to demand repayment.

00:08:52.084 --> 00:09:10.284
<v Casey>The sequence has a second half. CTV reports that ISC is moving to collect, and has threatened to claw back all current funding if FSIN doesn't repay the $12 million flagged in the audit. That doesn't touch a ledger from years ago. It reaches the services running this year, and the people who rely on them this winter.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:27.856
<v Riley>Which is why the audit trail is the defence. A nation that keeps its own ledgers, with approvals and receipts attached to each expense, can answer a review line by line. Indigenous governments should be building transparent financial systems of their own, digital audit trail included.

00:09:28.206 --> 00:09:30.510
<v Casey>Who keeps that audit trail running in year five?

00:09:30.769 --> 00:09:43.097
<v Riley>The nation does, if the system is its own and not a vendor's. A vendor can change the rules, shut the service down, or raise the licence fee. A nation's own system can't be switched off by a contract renewal.

00:09:43.725 --> 00:09:59.717
<v Casey>Vendor lock-in is a real risk. It doesn't touch this dispute, though. The dispute is about who decides what counts as eligible. The department that issued the audit decision is the same department now moving to collect. A better spreadsheet doesn't change which office gets the final word.

00:10:00.257 --> 00:10:09.801
<v Riley>Then the courts are the forum. FSIN's legal action tests the number outside the department that made the call. Clean records give the lawyers something to check.

00:10:10.311 --> 00:10:19.727
<v Casey>Courts take years. The clawback threat sits on current funding, and that doesn't wait for a judge. A nation can have clean records and still lose a program in the spring.

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:34.346
<v Riley>Which argues for a funding structure that doesn't leave a nation that exposed. That's a design question, and it doesn't replace the audit trail. Records are what let a nation win an argument about the number, and a judge needs a number to rule on.

00:10:34.881 --> 00:10:40.625
<v Casey>Then I want the design question answered first. Who writes the eligibility rules in the funding agreement?

00:10:40.916 --> 00:10:59.372
<v Riley>The federal government, in most contribution agreements. That's where the sovereignty claim gets tested. If FSIN says it's sovereign, can it accept a federal review as a condition of funding? Accepting the review isn't accepting the conclusion, and a nation can contest a finding without refusing the process.

00:10:59.971 --> 00:11:19.379
<v Casey>Sounds clean. But the money moves through agreements on an Indian Act-era contribution framework. The sovereignty claim and the funding relationship don't sit on the same legal footing, and FSIN's public position leans hard on sovereignty. Claiming one and accepting the other creates friction, and it lands on the people using the services.

00:11:19.904 --> 00:11:38.144
<v Riley>It can be both, and that's uncomfortable. A nation can assert sovereignty over its own decisions and still sign agreements for federal money, as long as the terms are written down and it knows which clauses it's trading away. Most friction in these disputes comes from terms nobody read closely at signing.

00:11:38.767 --> 00:11:52.383
<v Casey>Nobody has shown us those terms. We're working from published reports, not the audit. What ISC considers ineligible is in a document we haven't read, so I won't guess what the $12 million paid for. The reports even disagree on the figure.

00:11:53.021 --> 00:11:59.189
<v Riley>Careful is right. But a nation that wants its own books taken seriously should be able to show the receipts without a fight.

00:11:59.704 --> 00:12:04.584
<v Casey>Show them to whom? The department, and the department is the party that wants the money back.

00:12:05.484 --> 00:12:32.900
<v Riley>Different thread, about objects rather than money. The Kawacatoose tipi cover came back to Kawacatoose First Nation in Saskatchewan after a century away. It belonged to sîpiskwêsîkan, and it was taken in 1926. It had been held at the Smithsonian Institution in Washington, D.C. Chief Cory Dustyhorn, a descendant, was the first to touch the painted cover when it came home. The return came after the community met with Smithsonian representatives.

00:12:33.507 --> 00:12:43.395
<v Casey>Notice the order. The tool didn't bring it back. A meeting did. The community sat across from the institution, and the institution agreed to hand over an object it had held for a hundred years.

00:12:43.905 --> 00:12:58.377
<v Riley>Digital cataloguing, 3D documentation, and virtual access could help other nations find their objects and ask for them back. A catalogue tells a nation what's held and where. A nation that doesn't know an object exists can't ask for it at all.

00:12:58.986 --> 00:13:10.970
<v Casey>The central fact is a physical object held for a century without consent. A scan is still a scan. Digital access is no substitute for return, and I'd be uneasy if a museum offered a viewing in place of the cover.

00:13:11.485 --> 00:13:19.221
<v Riley>Nobody is proposing the scan as a substitute. Finding the object is the first step, and a catalogue makes the request possible.

00:13:19.742 --> 00:13:30.230
<v Casey>But the request is the old work. Restitution is a conversation with the institution that holds the thing, then a decision to hand it over. The database doesn't make the institution decide.

00:13:30.875 --> 00:13:38.331
<v Riley>I'll still call the search innovation. The tool locates the object, and the meeting gets it home. The second depends on the first.

00:13:38.942 --> 00:13:47.430
<v Casey>Call it restitution. And the meeting took a century to happen, which the innovation framing leaves out. If I'd been the cover, I'd have wanted a faster courier.

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:54.360
<v Riley>A century in a climate-controlled drawer. Even the Smithsonian's filing system was faster than its conscience.

00:13:54.898 --> 00:14:28.928
<v Casey>Now the dates, because a residential school timeline is where a shaky number does real damage. The Eagle Feather News piece says the Gordon and Muscowequan residential schools were running through much of the 1990s. The Regina Leader-Post reports that the Lebret school closed on June 30th, 1998, after 114 years. The National Centre for Truth and Reconciliation lists Lebret as operating from 1884 to 1998. For Muscowequan, CBC reports the Catholic church ran the school until the federal government took it over in 1969.

00:14:29.592 --> 00:14:46.448
<v Riley>So the Lebret date is solid, and it supports the final-generation framing. That framing challenges the story that residential schools all closed in the 1970s and 80s. The 1990s claim for Gordon and Muscowequan isn't settled, and it's better to say that plainly.

00:14:47.135 --> 00:14:58.063
<v Casey>Testimony is evidence. It isn't a timeline until someone checks it against the records. The survivors' accounts are powerful, and the dates still need sourcing before anybody repeats them as fact.

00:14:58.681 --> 00:15:09.689
<v Riley>The dates matter in the fight over the Conservative candidate accused of residential school denialism, which we had on Wednesday. A denier will look for any wrong date to argue from.

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:17.664
<v Casey>Only if the dates don't survive a check. Otherwise the denier gets easier ground, and the survivors lose the argument over a detail nobody verified.

00:15:18.308 --> 00:15:26.932
<v Riley>Eagle Feather is publishing survivor accounts in their own words. Who holds that record, and who can change it? This show doesn't have the answer yet.

00:15:27.616 --> 00:15:40.872
<v Casey>The newsroom doesn't own the accounts, and ownership is the question I'd keep asking. The Dene leaders in the education resolution are asking for the same thing at the child-welfare table: decisions made by the people the system is meant to serve.

00:15:41.463 --> 00:15:55.879
<v Riley>The Cariboo version is the Tŝilhqot'in title decision in 2014. The Supreme Court recognized title over a defined territory. That's a legal foundation many nations don't have, and it binds in a way a funding agreement doesn't.

00:15:56.457 --> 00:16:09.265
<v Casey>Title took decades of litigation and specific facts about occupation. Those aren't replicable for every nation. And the title ruling doesn't pay a caseworker in Williams Lake or Quesnel. Someone still has to do that job every day.

00:16:09.881 --> 00:16:29.769
<v Riley>So there are two models. Legal title is slow but binding. Community-held services are faster but depend on who holds the records and the staff. Denisiqi Services Society is the second model, and it has delivered culturally grounded child and family services for Tŝilhqot'in and Ulkatcho communities for more than twenty years.

00:16:30.318 --> 00:16:45.558
<v Casey>That's the one I'd test. Denisiqi answers to its communities, which is the governance point. But a service like that needs funded staff who stay, and case files only it controls. If the funder changes the reporting rules, does the service answer to the families, or to the form?

00:16:45.865 --> 00:16:56.953
<v Riley>Does the service keep its own records, set its own standards, and report to its own communities first? If yes, it's governed by the people it serves, and a funder's form is secondary.

00:16:57.539 --> 00:17:07.379
<v Casey>The form is the problem. Every funder asks for numbers, and the numbers get shaped by whoever writes the questions. Programs drift toward whatever the reporting template rewards.

00:17:08.054 --> 00:17:28.166
<v Riley>Then hold the template too. Let the nation write the reporting fields, and let the funder read them. That costs far less than a court case. It also answers who gets to stay: a young person who comes home from the city to teach language from a digital archive is staying faithful to the community only if the community owns the archive.

00:17:28.753 --> 00:17:40.537
<v Casey>Better fix than most. I still don't know whether a sovereignty claim survives a clawback, and I'd like to see that tested in a courtroom, not a press release. A clawback that reaches current services is the test.

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:52.796
<v Riley>It survives if the nation holds its own records and writes its own reporting terms. The FSIN case will show whether that holds, and nations that build the records now will be ready either way.

00:17:53.348 --> 00:18:01.260
<v Casey>My residual worry is the same one. Who can change the reporting terms after the money goes out? Neither we nor the reports we read have that answer.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:15.816
<v Riley>So the thing to carry out is who holds the audit trail, and whether a sovereignty claim can survive a clawback. If you've got a correction, a story tip, or a record we should check, send it to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We'd like to hear it.

00:18:16.111 --> 00:18:20.015
<v Casey>And check the Lebret date yourself before you repeat it. Talk tomorrow.

00:18:20.329 --> 00:18:21.809
<v Riley>Have a great rest of your day, everyone.

00:18:22.409 --> 00:18:32.513
<v Casey>We haven't had a chance to talk directly with anyone from First Nations communications teams for today's episode, and that's a conversation we'd really welcome, so if that's you, reach out.